{{!< default}}
{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     post.hbs — THE DISPATCHER, not a page.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Ghost renders every post through this one template, so the kind of page a
     post becomes is decided here, by its internal tag. Getting it wrong
     renders every post as the wrong kind of page — which is why it is second
     in abstract/ after the content model itself.

     THE RULE: one branch per collection, each delegating to exactly one
     partial in partials/post/, and NO markup in this file. The moment a
     branch grows its own layout, the two collections sharing it start to
     drift.

     ══ THREE VOCABULARIES, ON PURPOSE AND TEMPORARILY ══════════════════════
     Every branch matches every name a post might carry, because three
     naming schemes are alive at once and all three are real:

       published      canonical (registry)   target (bright's -col)
       ─────────────  ────────────────────   ──────────────────────
       #training-section  #training-module   #training-sections-col
       #training-content  #training-lesson   #training-lessons-col
       #course            #course            #courses-col
       #lesson            #lesson            #lessons-col
       #blog              #blog              #blog-col

     The right column is bright's own vocabulary, taken from the seeded
     content rather than invented — and PLURAL for children, which matters:
     #lessons-col belongs to courses, so training's children must be
     #training-lessons-col or the two collections merge.

     The middle column was a rename decision 0004 recorded and never carried
     out, on the premise that "the theme has no content yet" — false then and
     false now. The right column is the convention being migrated TO.

     Matching all three is what lets the retag be additive and reversible:
     scripts/migrate-col-tags.mjs ADDS the -col tags and removes nothing, so
     old routes.yaml and new both work while it runs. When the retag is done
     and the new routes.yaml is uploaded, delete the first two columns from
     these branches — `grep -rn "hash-training-section\|hash-blog\b" .` is
     the list. Nothing else in the theme needs to know.

     ⚠ ORDER MATTERS. Training is matched BEFORE the generic branches: a
     lesson is also a post with a primary tag, and a generic branch placed
     first would claim it.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#post}}
    {{#has tag="#training-lessons-col, #training-lesson, #training-content"}}
        {{> "post/training-lesson"}}
    {{else has tag="#training-sections-col, #training-module, #training-section"}}
        {{> "post/training-module"}}
    {{else has tag="#lessons-col, #lesson"}}
        {{> "post/lesson"}}
    {{else has tag="#courses-col, #course"}}
        {{> "post/course"}}
    {{else has tag="#folders-col, #docs-section"}}
        {{> "post/docs-section"}}
    {{else has tag="#docs-col, #docs-page"}}
        {{> "post/docs"}}
    {{else}}
        {{> "post/article"}}
    {{/has}}
{{/post}}
